  "query_preview": "../Saturday through Tuesday/...\nAn active weather pattern will persist across SEAK through the \nforecast period. Multiple fronts/lows will push through the Gulf \nmove into the panhandle over the weekend and into early next week.\nThe big player that will send the fronts into the Gulf and \nthrough SEAK will be the remnants of Typhoon Dujuan and associated\nupper- level low/trough that will slowly swing through southern \ninterior into the Gulf through the weekend into early next week. \nBetween each front, weaker moist onshore flow will keep mostly \ncloudy skies and showers. The initial front will lift \nnortheastward through SEAK on Saturday, followed quickly by a \nsecondary stronger front pushing east later Saturday night and \nSunday. A period of moderate to heavy rain and gusty S-SEly winds \nare expected with each frontal passage, especially with the Sunday\nsystem as deeper moisture feed ahead of front will bring higher \nprecipitation rates. Cooler temperatures aloft with the upper \ntrough will generate modest instability, bringing a potential for \nisolated thunderstorms, mainly along the Gulf coast. By early next\nweek, guidance continues to indicate the active pattern \ncontinuing as the broad upper-level troughing remains over or just\nwest of the Gulf, with an additional front/low lifting northeast \nthrough the Gulf into the Panhandle Monday into Tuesday, keeping \nthe potential for moderate to heavy rain and gusty winds. Some \nguidance is quite aggressive with this low and bringing gales into\nthe Gulf. While model spread and uncertainly remains in regards \nto the timing, location and strength of this system, overall \nconfidence is high for a wet and active period. While rounds of \nmoderate to heavy rainfall are expected through the period, with \naround a 10-15% probability of precip rates reaching 6hr/24hr \n2-year return interval, each system is fairly progressive and a \nprolonged duration of heavy rain is not expected. The rainy and \ncloudy pattern will keep temperatures seasonably cool with highs \ngenerally in the 50s.",
